\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Systems of particular interest: every sample system within 15\,pc hosting at least one confirmed planet, ordered by distance. $N_{\rm  pl}$ is the confirmed-planet count, $N_{\rm  A}$ the number of Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows, EIRP$_{90}$ the deepest sensitivity the survey reached for the system, and $N_{\rm  eb}$ the number of primary-census execution blocks covering the system, so $N_{\rm  eb}>1$ means repeat epochs exist within the census. Of these 15{} systems, 6{} have no Class\nobreakspace  {}A coverage and so carry no drift-resolving limit, only the coarse-channel limit marked B --- the archival selection of \S  \ref  {sec:bias} acting on exactly the targets a transmitter search would choose. The full target list is in the data release.}}{6}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:interesting}{{3}{6}{Systems of particular interest: every sample system within 15\,pc hosting at least one confirmed planet, ordered by distance. $N_{\rm pl}$ is the confirmed-planet count, $N_{\rm A}$ the number of Class~A windows, EIRP$_{90}$ the deepest sensitivity the survey reached for the system, and $N_{\rm eb}$ the number of primary-census execution blocks covering the system, so $N_{\rm eb}>1$ means repeat epochs exist within the census. Of these \CsIntNRow {} systems, \CsIntNoClassA {} have no Class~A coverage and so carry no drift-resolving limit, only the coarse-channel limit marked B --- the archival selection of \S \ref {sec:bias} acting on exactly the targets a transmitter search would choose. The full target list is in the data release}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the 402{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz to 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation at the equator (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} TRAPPIST--1{} windows. For the most demanding case, transiting TRAPPIST-1\nobreakspace  {}b, 29{} per cent of the orbital phase lies above the default ceiling and 1{} per cent above the widened one.}}{6}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{4}{6}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz to \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation at the equator (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is also the median; it runs to \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} \AccelWideStar {} windows. For the most demanding case, transiting TRAPPIST-1~b, \TrapPhaseHi {} per cent of the orbital phase lies above the default ceiling and \TrapPhaseLo {} per cent above the widened one}{figure.4}{}}
